    "translation": "Woman: Today we talk with Mr. Fujii, the master brewer at a two-hundred-year-old sake brewery. Thank you for joining us. / Man: Thank you. / Woman: The toji is the person in charge of sake brewing, isn't he? Is it a world where the intuition of long years decides everything? / Man: That's what people used to say. But at our brewery, ten years ago we started recording everything in numbers — tank temperatures, the state of fermentation. / Woman: You brought data into a traditional world. Was there no opposition? / Man: There was. The older craftsmen told me again and again that sake is something you brew by intuition. / Woman: Do you not believe in intuition, Mr. Fujii? / Man: No — the opposite. Intuition is long years of experience accumulated inside the body — data inside the body, so to speak. That's why it's the real thing. The problem is that only the person himself can use it. / Woman: And if you record it, it can be passed to others? / Man: Exactly. In the old days they said \"steal the craft by watching,\" and it took twenty years to become a full craftsman. But if records remain, young people can learn much faster. / Woman: So the records exist to hand down the craft quickly. / Man: Yes. With craftsmen dwindling, if we kept the techniques secret, sake brewing itself would disappear. It is precisely to preserve the tradition that a new way of doing things is needed. / Woman: Thank you very much for today. / Man: Thank you.",
